<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="401" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110268" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19.jpeg 1267w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-300x143.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-1024x489.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-768x367.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>My algo for <a screenshot=true href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2025/01/18/maps-wordpress-statistics/">maps</a> sucked because there was just too big a difference between the top country and the rest:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-3.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-3.jpeg" alt="" width="477" height="544" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110330" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-3.jpeg 477w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-3-263x300.jpeg 263w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 477px) 100vw, 477px" /></a></p>
<p>So my map looked like this:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="399" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110269" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-1.jpeg 1250w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-1-300x143.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-1-1024x487.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-1-768x366.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>See?  The US is white while the rest of the countries are basically two different hues (non-visited countries and visited countries).</p>
<p>Then I remembered that logarithms were a thing that existed, and presto!</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="401" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110268" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19.jpeg 1267w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-300x143.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-1024x489.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-19-768x367.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a><br />
Look how pretty!</p>
<p>I amaze myself.</p>
<p>Anyway, that world map I used had a kinda wonky coding &#8212; it mixed using <tt>id</tt> country codes for contiguous countries and <tt>class</tt> country names for non-contiguous ones.  So I&#8217;ve straightened that up and put the result on <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/world-map" data-cached-time="2025-01-19T20:31:24" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/ewpb4Y9tV.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">Microsoft Github</a> as a separate package, if you should ever need a world map like that.</p>
<p>That is, you can say:</p>
<pre>.US {
  fill: red;
}
</pre>
<p>This didn&#8217;t work in the original version, and you had to mangle the SVG structure itself.</p>
<p>Anyway.</p>
